American football is a team sport which typically corresponds to what the rest of the world refers to as "football". Other common names for American football are gridiron,^{[1]} footyball^{[2]}^{[3]} or simply football.^{[4]}

Players on each team attempt to drive a four-to-five pound (1.85–2.2 kg) inflated football over a 100 yard (91 m) long field into end zone portions of a U-shaped pair of goal lines. Scored touches are called touchdowns and are worth six points. A players' football skills include tossing, kicking and carrying the ball, as well as tackling or blocking other players from getting the ball to the end zones.

American football evolved from the need of rugby and soccer to have a player tackle the ball carrier in midair before scoring a try or a goal. Since its creation in the late 19th century, this sport has become the most popular sports in the United States, where it is currently the only major league whose champions are determined by a playoff system rather than directly through the season record.
